        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          <div>OBJECTIVES:</div><div><div>Long-duration missions under weightless conditions have resulted in notable changes to the function and structure of the eyes in some astronauts. This phenomenon, Spaceflight-Associated Neuro-ocular Syndrome (SANS), is hypothesized to develop in response to chronic exposure to the headward fluid shifts that occur in the spaceflight environment. The headward fluid shift increases congestion of the veins that drain the head, leading to impaired cerebrospinal and lymphatic fluid drainage from the head and changes to the eye. Countermeasures are being proposed to counteract these effects, however the amount of partial gravity required to re-establish footward fluid shift has not yet been identified. Parabolic flight provides a unique opportunity to evaluate the acute changes associated with varying levels of gravity (G-levels) and describe how important venous parameters change at levels less than normal Earth gravity.</div><div>This study has three specific aims:</div><div>(1) Determine the relationship between G-level and upper body venous hemodynamic parameters associated with a headward fluid shift, including internal jugular vein (IJV) pressure, flow pattern, and cross-sectional area.</div><div>(2) Assess if the relationship between G-level and upper body venous hemodynamic parameters is influenced by differences in anatomy between the left and right sides of the body.</div><div>(3) Determine whether the supine posture in normal gravity is an appropriate analog for weightlessness with respect to internal jugular vein hemodynamics.</div></div><div><br></div><div>APPROACH:</div><div><div>Internal jugular vein cross-sectional area, pressure, and flow pattern will be assessed in nine subjects across a range of G-levels and postures using ultrasound. Conditions will include: 1.0-G supine (preflight), 1.0-G seated (preflight), 0.0-G (parabolic flight), 0.25-G seated (parabolic flight), 0.50-G seated (parabolic flight), 0.75-G seated (parabolic flight), and 1.0-G seated (level flight). All participants will be between 25 and 55 years old, and between 157-183 cm tall. All IJV measurements will be collected bilaterally.</div><div>The aircraft will fly in parabolas that initially produce increased gravity (1.8-G, “hypergravity”), followed by ~20-50 second periods of partial gravity or weightlessness. After that, there will be another pull-up (hypergravity) to complete the parabola. The plane then will fly in normal horizontal flight for a few minutes before repeating the parabolic flight path again. Subjects will participate in two parabolic flights: one producing partial gravity and one producing weightlessness during each parabola. Each flight will include 31 parabolas, with the first parabola&nbsp;serving as a “practice” parabola to verify equipment operation. Then six sets of five parabolas will be performed. During the partial gravity flights, subjects will experience three different G-levels (0.25-G, 0.50-G, and 0.75-G) with at least 10 parabolas at each G-level. The order of the G-levels will not be randomly assigned within or across days of flight but will differ across days. During the flight(s) that produce weightlessness, the IJV parameters will be acquired for each subject across 10 parabolas. Accelerometer data (from an accelerometer affixed to an equipment rack) will be used to identify the respective partial-gravity and weightlessness epochs that participants experience during data collection. Baseline data will be collected at 1.00-G preflight in the supine and seated position and at 1.00-G seated during level flight.</div></div><div><br></div><div>RESULTS:</div><div>The investigators anticipate this research will expand the current knowledge base on the effects of IJV anatomy on hemodynamic variables believed to be associated with the risk of SANS and venous thrombosis during varying G-levels. Specifically, this research will fully describe the IJV pressure, cross-sectional area, and flow characterization during normal upright sitting in 1.00-G (normal Earth conditions), across a range of G-levels (including 0.00-G), and while supine as a 0.00-G analog. The investigators also seek to validate IJV hemodynamics during supine 1.00-G as an accurate model for IJV hemodynamics during weightlessness. Altogether, the results of this research will (1) inform the level of fluid shift required to reverse the hemodynamic effects of weightlessness, (2) guide the implementation of a SANS and/or venous thrombosis countermeasure during space flight, and (3) inform whether the gravitational levels on the Moon (0.16-G) and Mars (0.38-G) will be protective against these health risks.</div>
        </div>

